WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:01.940
Hey babe, good news!

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:04.560
I just signed us up for a new retirement plan!

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.560
Oh!

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.560
...It’s a lottery ticket.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.580
I know, it’s only like a one in a million
chance, but I’ve got a good feeling about this one!

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.440
Try one in 292 million.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:18.020
When probabilities get that small, our brains
have trouble processing them…

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.690
But for reference, you’re more likely to
become an astronaut, or be killed in a vending

00:00:21.690 --> 00:00:22.690
machine accident.

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.650
And according to some people, even if you
win, you might end up wishing a vending machine

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.280
had taken you out instead.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:37.860
Let’s start with the basics.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.010
What IS the lottery?

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:41.610
Essentially, it’s government-run gambling.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:47.390
Kings, Emperors and presidents alike realized
that people hate taxes but love to gamble.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:52.780
So lotteries were implemented to keep revenue
flowing in without causing the peasants to revolt.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.700
For more ancient lottery history, let’s
check in with Danielle from Origin of Everything!

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:03.960
The earliest known evidence of lottery-style
gambling dates back between 205 and 187 BCE

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.970
during the Han Dynasty in China.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.540
It was actually in the form of a game now
called Keno that’s still played in some

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:10.970
casinos today!

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:14.860
And similar to modern times, most of the revenue
went to funding government-projects.

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:18.390
Because the money for expanding and maintaining that big old wall needed to come from

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:19.220
somewhere!

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:23.520
Lottery tickets first showed up in the
Roman Empire under Julius Ceasar’s successor

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.520
Augustus.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.960
He too needed a little extra cash to fix up
some infrastructure.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.400
But the prizes didn’t come in the form of
money instead winners got materials

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.660
like household items.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.840
And bookoo payouts similar to what we have today
didn’t arrive until the 15th century in

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:38.890
the Low Countries.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:41.420
Today we know that region as “The Netherlands.”

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:47.010
Which makes sense since the word lottery actually
comes from the Dutch word loterij, the word

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.420
“lot" actually means “fate”.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.240
Believe it or not, lotteries played a significant
role in the establishment of America

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.729
The proceeds from a lottery in 1612 were essential
in funding the establishment of Jamestown

00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:00.490
by the Virginia Company.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:04.590
And these days almost every state in the US
runs a lottery, and hundreds of other countries

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:05.590
continue the practice.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:07.170
So what’s the harm?

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.980
Surely something that’s so widespread can’t
be that bad right?!

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.000
Let’s start with the obvious.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.170
For 99.9999999% of people who play, the lottery
is a waste of money.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:24.240
And even though most people know that the
statistical odds of winning are horrendous,

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.800
people continue to play in droves.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.470
In fact, 60% of adults report playing at least
once a year.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.680
But unfortunately the most dedicated players tend
to be poor.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:41.260
Households with incomes of less than
$12,400, on average, spend 5% of their income

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:42.270
on lottery tickets.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.980
And this is no accident.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.780
Studies show that Lottery ads are specifically
targeted to low income groups.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.069
Which is kind of perverse when you consider
that the lottery is essentially a voluntary

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:57.780
tax which is falling most heavily on the shoulders
of the people who can least afford it.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.430
And there are ways that the government could
regulate the industry to keep it from being

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.870
such a raw deal.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.349
In Ireland they have something called a “prize
bond” which essentially means players can

00:03:07.349 --> 00:03:12.240
be entered into the drawing simply by loaning
the government a couple bucks, which can be

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.360
refunded on demand.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.340
And where is all the revenue from the lottery
going anyway?!

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:21.030
It’s kind of hard to say because even though
it’s essentially a tax, the revenue is not

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:24.630
subject to the kind of scrutiny that real
taxes are.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:29.040
Most states have it earmarked for purposes
meant to benefit the public good like education

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.470
or park land restoration, but there’s evidence
that suggests the increased revenue just ends

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:35.800
up being a shell game.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:40.740
It’s not uncommon for state legislators
to cut back funding to the exact programs

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:46.450
the lottery was designed to help, most notably
education, and just increasing budgets elsewhere.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.320
Finally, let’s address the core issue.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.740
People aren’t playing the lottery because
they think they’re helping their fellow

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:56.210
man, but because they believe that the payout
will make their lives better.

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.300
So, does it?

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.879
There have been lots of studies done on lottery
winners over the last few decades.

00:04:00.879 --> 00:04:03.090
And sometimes their conclusions conflict.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:07.519
No, not everyone ends up miserable or buried
under cement somewhere.

00:04:07.519 --> 00:04:13.620
But large unearned flushes of cash, also known
as “sudden income shock” can have significantly

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:16.889
detrimental effects on physical and mental
health.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:21.150
One study showed that although winners’
life satisfaction improved, their overall

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:23.180
happiness did not.

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.449
In his book “Money for Nothing” Edward
Ugel interviewed thousands of lottery winners

00:04:27.449 --> 00:04:31.190
and said “You’d be shocked at how many
wish they’d never won”.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:35.740
This is possibly due to something called the
hedonic treadmill theory!

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:40.990
This is an all too familiar phenomenon where
as a person makes more money, their expectations

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:46.210
and desires rise in tandem, which results
in no permanent gain in happiness.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:50.300
So if you were happy and satisfied in the
first place, the lottery won’t change that

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:51.330
very much.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:55.729
And if you were unhappy before winning, a
massive cash increase can exacerbate your

00:04:55.729 --> 00:04:56.729
problem.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:04:58.259
So is it BAD to play the lottery?

00:04:58.259 --> 00:04:59.319
Well, yeah!

00:04:59.319 --> 00:05:04.400
It’s objectively a waste of money that feeds
an exploitative system which relies on our

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:07.539
collective participation, no matter how small.

00:05:07.539 --> 00:05:10.639
BUT that doesn’t mean that dreaming about
winning is bad.

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:14.900
As a matter of fact, our lottery fantasies
can be a really revealing.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:18.199
What’s the first thing you picture when
you imagine winning?

00:05:18.199 --> 00:05:21.129
Do you fantasize about quitting that job you
hate?

00:05:21.129 --> 00:05:23.119
Donating to a certain charity?

00:05:23.119 --> 00:05:26.210
Starting that artisanal ice cream shop you’ve
always dreamed of?

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.819
Well, you don’t need to win the lottery
to get started on those goals.

00:05:29.819 --> 00:05:35.719
The next time you hear about the newest massive
payout, pay attention to where your mind goes.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:40.540
Most likely those wild and crazy dreams have
something totally achievable at their core.

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.400
And that’s our two cents.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:48.280
Special thanks to Danielle Bainbridge from Origin of Everything for contributing to the episode.

